(SETF (THE type place) value) is unsupported
============================================

SYMPTOM
-------
    (let ((v (vector 1 2 3)))
      (setf (the fixnum (aref v 0)) 9) v)
    => SETF: no expander for place operator THE

CLHS 5.1.2.6: THE is a valid place.  (setf (the value-type place) value)
must store VALUE (of type VALUE-TYPE) into PLACE.  The standard expansion is
simply

    (setf (the type place) value)  ==  (setf place (the type value))

WHY THE GENERIC PATH FAILS
--------------------------
bocl's SETF binds every place-operator argument to a gensym temp and calls the
registered expander with those temps (prelude.lisp, the `(expander …)` cond
branch).  That is correct for places whose args are all VALUES (e.g. AREF's
index), but THE's first argument is a TYPE SPECIFIER (not evaluable) and its
second argument must remain a PLACE (not be pre-evaluated into a temp).  So a
plain DEFINE-SETF-EXPANDER cannot express it — THE needs a dedicated clause in
the SETF macro that rewrites (setf (the type place) v) to
(setf place (the type v)).

Discovered 2026-07-24 as the next blocker for bug-cl-ppcre after the
adjustable-string type-system fix (cl-ppcre's api.lisp uses SETF of THE).
